WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.219
Let's go next to our caller from the three
one five area code.

00:00:03.219 --> 00:00:05.740
Who's calling today from three one five.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.990
Hi David.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.260
This is Janice.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:16.090
I see a lot of stats about, uh, climate change
and automation.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:21.539
And I see that they, uh, the worst effects
of them happen to coincide around the same

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:25.230
decades around the 2040s and 2050s.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:31.260
And I'm wondering, do you think that a liberal
democracy or Western civilization or whatever

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.489
you call it, uh, how confident are you that
these things are gonna Survive?

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:39.600
Uh, things like automation and climate change?

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.510
Well, I think there's a bigger question you're
asking in general, which is it can feel for

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:52.280
us right now in the middle of it, like liberal
democracy and the sort of type of geopolitical

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.550
status quo that we have is just the way things
are going to be indefinitely.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.390
And then as soon as you just zoom out a little
bit, you see the impermanence of just about

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.519
everything that has existed.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:12.100
And so my doubts about being able to say x
or y is going to survive beyond a particular

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.570
point is that in general we just have a huge
bias towards believing that the way things

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.100
are now are the way things are going to remain.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.619
It's very hard for us to imagine how things
could be drastically different.

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:26.560
But you know, like Rome fell eventually.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.549
And if history has taught us anything to the
extent that we know history, it's that even

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:32.869
things that seem permanent are not.

00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:38.630
So as far as climate change specifically,
there are a number of different theories and

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.469
ideas about how quickly things are going to
happen, automation, et Cetera.

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:48.529
The truth is we're pretty damn bad as a species
at predicting what is going to be going on

00:01:48.529 --> 00:01:50.779
beyond like five years at least.

00:01:50.779 --> 00:01:54.639
Societaly now scientific predictions are different.

00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:57.959
Things move at a glacial pace, no pun intended.

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:03.759
Um, but even still, I don't, I think that
what we're identifying now are trends and

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:08.970
the specificity that is sometimes attributed
is maybe a little overzealous in terms of

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.430
how good we are at predicting things because
there will be inflection points that continue

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:15.660
to kind of like modify their trajectory.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.329
But I think with all of these questions there
are real, it's a serious open question as

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:27.530
to whether a humanity will survive as we know
it, beyond, you know, relatively modest periods

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:28.620
of time.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:35.019
Well, I'm not worried so much about the survival
of the human race, just the, uh, I guess all

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:37.989
the progress we've made in the past say 200
years.

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:39.550
Yeah, they're there.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:44.040
It's completely reasonable to think that there
could be a significant backslide.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.329
There's a lot of speculative fiction that
has explored ways in which it would happen,

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:54.430
where we would effectively, as you're saying,
go back to the way things were 150 or 200

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.440
years ago.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.480
And I mean, I just don't know the, you know,
the, the reason that these questions are hard

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.939
for me is like, I can just speculate, but
I just know that we're terrible at this stuff

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:03.959
at predicting the stuff.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:04.959
Like, I just don't know.

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:06.180
I don't think we know.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.140
All right.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.140
Thank you.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.140
All right.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.140
Appreciate the phone call.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:11.144
Thank you so much.

